    <body>
      <sec id="article-27441.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Porphyria cutanea tarda (PCT) is a disorder within a spectrum of porphyrias, a group of enzymatic diseases that&#x000a0;cause&#x000a0;abnormal heme biosynthesis. It is caused by the deficiency of the uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase (UROD) enzyme and should be differentiated from other porphyrias. The concurrent presence of iron dysmetabolism with overload and the sequelae that follow makes it essential to search for other concomitant illnesses that may exhibit the same abnormality. The predisposing factors are many and can overlap. PCT is a nonfatal condition that typically presents with skin manifestations, provided it is appropriately diagnosed and managed in a timely and consistent manner. This activity outlines the evaluation and management of porphyria cutanea tarda, emphasizing its relationship to other predisposing illnesses; it also highlights the role of the interprofessional team in improving care for patients with this condition.&#x000a0;&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify and explain the etiology of PCT.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Assess the presentation of a patient with PCT.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Compare the management considerations for patients with PCT.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement a strategy integrating the interprofessional team in order to educate, treat, and monitor patients with PCT.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=27441&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=27441">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27441.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Porphyrias are a spectrum of metabolic disorders arising from a defect or alteration of enzymes in the heme biosynthesis pathway.&#x000a0;The heme pathway starts with the synthesis of D-aminolevulinic acid from succinyl-CoA and glycine.&#x000a0;Porphyrias&#x000a0;manifest with neurovisceral symptoms, cutaneous symptoms, or a combination of both. Porphyria cutanea tarda (PCT), the most common porphyria, is the result of a deficiency of the enzyme&#x000a0;uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase (UROD). The hallmark of this condition is photosensitivity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r2">[2]</xref> Hepatic UROD catalyzes the conversion of uroporphyrinogen III to coproporphyrinogen III. Failure of this process results in the accumulation of upstream compounds in the liver that eventually appear in the plasma and urine.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;Hepatoerythropoietic porphyria (HEP) is another rare form of porphyria that is clinically similar to PCT but results from a homozygous UROD&#x000a0;mutation, whereas UROD mutations in PCT, when present, are typically heterozygous.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27441.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>Porphyria cutanea tarda arises from reduced or absent uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase activity, resulting in accumulation of highly carboxylated porphyrinogens, including uroporphyrinogen. These compounds are oxidized to their corresponding porphyrins, including uroporphyrin and heptacarboxyl porphyrins. Deposition of these toxic compounds in the liver and skin contributes to the resulting clinical manifestations. Clinical manifestations typically do not occur until enzymatic activity falls below 20% of normal.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Classification of PCT</bold>
</p>
        <p>Type I PCT, the sporadic form of the condition, is the most common type, accounting for 80% of cases. Although UROD enzyme levels are normal, its activity is reduced in the liver, likely due to a UROD inhibitor and susceptibility factors.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r3">[3]</xref> Type II PCT is the familial form, inherited in an autosomal-dominant pattern, and accounts for approximately 20% of cases. Unlike type I, this form is characterized by a roughly 50% reduction in UROD enzymatic activity across all cells. Because 20% activity is sufficient for normal function, additional environmental factors or a UROD inhibitor are generally required for symptoms to develop, which explains the low penetrance and why most gene carriers remain asymptomatic.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r6">[6]</xref> Approximately 110 UROD mutations have been identified (60% missense and 40% nonsense, deletions, or insertions), with about 75% located between exons 5 and 10.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r7">[7]</xref> Type III PCT is a rare form similar to type I, with normal UROD genes; however, its occurrence in more than one family member suggests a genetic mechanism other than a UROD mutation.</p>
        <p>
<bold>UROD Inhibitor</bold>
</p>
        <p>The role of iron in the pathogenesis of PCT is well established, with alterations in iron metabolism (eg, iron overload and elevated ferritin levels) contributing to disease development.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r8">[8]</xref> Excess iron facilitates the formation of oxygen-free radicals, which promote the generation of a UROD inhibitor and enhance the photosensitizing potential of uroporphyrin.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r9">[9]</xref> Although low hepcidin levels can increase iron activity, this phase-reactive protein is difficult to assess in the presence of inflammation. In addition, other susceptibility factors are thought to contribute to the formation of UROD inhibitors.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Susceptibility Factors</bold>
</p>
        <p>Several factors can trigger the clinical manifestations of PCT. In most cases, a combination of two or more is required. Alcohol is a major contributor, as it downregulates hepcidin and increases iron absorption while also increasing aminolevulinic acid (ALA) synthase activity and inhibiting uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase. Clinical studies have shown that approximately 80% to 90% of patients with PCT manifestations report a history of heavy alcohol consumption.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r10">[10]</xref></p>
        <p>Hepatitis C virus (HCV) infection is another important factor. Several mechanisms have been proposed for how HCV contributes to PCT, including increased hepatocyte iron, elevated oxidative stress, decreased intracellular glutathione, and the formation of a UROD inhibitor. Notably, the pathogenesis is not due to reduced enzyme activity but rather direct hepatocellular damage.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r11">[11]</xref>&#x000a0;Clinical evidence suggests that PCT may be diagnosed up to six years before HCV is identified, and past studies have shown that successful antiviral treatment of HCV can resolve PCT manifestations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r14">[14]</xref></p>
        <p>Estrogen exposure is also linked to PCT, although the exact mechanism remains unclear. About 66% of PCT patients report exogenous estrogen use.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r15">[15]</xref> Certain forms, such as diethylstilbestrol, can induce ALA synthase and promote porphyrin accumulation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r16">[16]</xref> This issue is particularly relevant for transgender individuals using estrogen for phenotypic effects.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r17">[17]</xref> Patients under medical supervision may be advised to reduce or discontinue estrogen therapy to lower their PCT risk, while those obtaining estrogens from unregulated sources may not recognize the disease and may also engage in other risk behaviors, including smoking, alcohol use, or HIV infection. Consequently, affected individuals often face a difficult decision: discontinue estrogen therapy to resolve PCT or continue therapy and manage ongoing symptoms. &#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Approximately 13% of patients with PCT are infected with HIV.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r15">[15]</xref> Its role in PCT appears largely related to the high prevalence of concurrent HCV infection. Infection with HIV may disrupt the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis, lowering testosterone and leaving estrogen relatively dominant. Additionally, HIV can cause hepatic damage, impairing hormone and steroid metabolism and further exacerbating PCT in the presence of estrogen therapy.&#x000a0;&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Hemochromatosis is another significant factor, with up to 53% of PCT patients carrying a hemochromatosis gene mutation, most commonly C282Y and H63D.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r15">[15]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r18">[18]</xref>&#x000a0;The disease leads to hepcidin deficiency, resulting in increased iron absorption and mobilization. Excess iron stimulates uroporphyrin production and accumulation, contributing directly to PCT manifestations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r19">[19]</xref></p>
        <p>Smoking is highly prevalent among patients with PCT, with approximately 81% reporting a history of smoking. The mechanism is thought to involve induction of CYP1A2, which in turn promotes UROD inhibitor synthesis, thereby increasing disease risk. Finally, chemical porphyria presents with a clinical picture similar to PCT. A notable historical example occurred in Turkey during the 1950s famine, when thousands of children and adults developed porphyria after consuming wheat treated with the fungicide hexachlorobenzene.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r20">[20]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r21">[21]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27441.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Porphyria cutanea tarda is the most common type of porphyria worldwide.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;According to a Norwegian study, it has a prevalence of approximately 1 in 10,000 individuals&#x000a0;and occurs most commonly in middle-aged adults.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r22">[22]</xref>&#x000a0;A prevalence of 1 in 25,000 has been reported in the United States.&#x000a0;Porphyria cutanea tarda&#x000a0;usually affects both males and females equally. Some susceptibility factors tend to affect one sex more than the other, such as estrogen&#x000a0;therapy in women and alcohol use or HCV infection in men.&#x000a0;&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27441.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>The deficiency or absence of the enzyme uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase causes an accumulation of porphyrins. These porphyrins enter the bloodstream, where they interact with external light sources, particularly blue wavelengths around 410 nm. Exposure to light initially excites the porphyrin molecules, which then return to their ground state by releasing energy to molecular oxygen. This produces singlet-excited oxygen, which reacts with key biological molecules such as lipids and proteins, causing membrane lipid peroxidation, compromise of cell membrane structure, and ultimately cell death.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r23">[23]</xref> The oxidative damage to cell membranes and proteins manifests clinically as skin fragility and blister formation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r24">[24]</xref> Complement system activation can trigger mast cell degranulation and accumulation of transforming growth factor &#x003b2;, contributing to tissue sclerosis in PCT.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r25">[25]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27441.s6" sec-type="Histopathology">
        <title>Histopathology</title>
        <p>Cutaneous pathologic findings of PCT include subepidermal bullae and thick, hyalinized blood vessels in the upper dermis. Caterpillar bodies (linear globules that are periodic acid&#x02013;Schiff positive) are present at the roof of the blisters and represent disintegrating keratinocytes and basement membrane material. Dermal sclerosis may also&#x000a0;occur and can be histologically indistinguishable from scleroderma. Hepatic involvement may demonstrate focal steatosis, lobular necrosis with pigment-laden macrophages, portal inflammation (including triaditis and fibrosis), and periportal siderosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r21">[21]</xref> Porphyrin deposition can be visualized as red autofluorescence under ultraviolet light. Acicular inclusions, birefringent needle-like cytoplasmic structures, are characteristic of PCT and correspond to uroporphyrin accumulation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r26">[26]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27441.s7" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>Type I and type II PCT are clinically similar, and distinguishing between them is generally relevant only for management and patient counseling. A thorough history should assess exposure to known susceptibility factors, including alcohol use, smoking, and the use of chemical agents or estrogen. Attention should also be paid to any neurovisceral symptoms, which may suggest alternative porphyrias. Although family history is typically uninformative, a detailed medication history can help identify drug-related eruptions. Because porphyrin accumulation in PCT may cause reddish-brown discoloration of the urine, inquiries regarding urine color should be included in the clinical evaluation.</p>
        <p>Patients may present with facial hypertrichosis and, less commonly, corneosclerosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r27">[27]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r28">[28]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r29">[29]</xref> Photosensitive blistering lesions, erosions, and cysts typically occur on the dorsal hands, forearms, face, legs, and feet. The affected skin demonstrates increased fragility, often accompanied by mild scarring and atrophy. Photosensitivity is common, along with skin fragility and blister formation on sun-exposed areas. This most commonly involves the back of the hands, forearms, neck, face, and feet. These recurrent insults to the skin may result in residual hyper- and hypopigmented scarring and milia (small yellowish-white keratin-filled papules).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r16">[16]</xref> Because PCT is a delayed blistering photosensitivity disorder, most patients do not associate sun exposure with their skin lesions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r4">[4]</xref> The lesions often arise from minor trauma and may become secondarily infected, leading to&#x000a0;considerable pain and inflammation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r30">[30]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>A nonvirilizing type of hypertrichosis around the cheeks, temples, and forearms can be seen in women and may sometimes be the only physical manifestation of PCT. Scarring can occasionally lead to contractures, mimicking the cutaneous findings of scleroderma, a phenomenon known as pseudoscleroderma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r31">[31]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r32">[32]</xref> Examples of pseudoscleroderma include scleredema, which can occur after respiratory infections, and scleromyxedema, which is caused by mucinous accumulations. Scarring alopecia of the scalp may also occur, resulting in diffuse hair loss. Pigmentary changes can be observed in the facial, orbital, or malar regions, ranging from blue to purplish-brown hues.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r26">[26]</xref> Occasionally, an erythematous suffusion resembling the plethora seen in polycythemia vera may appear on sun-exposed areas.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r16">[16]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27441.s8" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>Evaluation of a patient with porphyria begins with measurement of porphyrin levels in urine or plasma. These levels are typically elevated in both&#x000a0;PCT and hepatoerythropoietic porphyria. Uroporphyrin and heptacarboxyl porphyrin are usually elevated, while urinary&#x000a0;ALA and porphobilinogen remain within normal limits. Measurement of porphyrin levels in feces is generally not required for diagnosis but can be useful in patients undergoing hemodialysis.</p>
        <p>Once elevated porphyrin levels are detected, further testing, including porphyrin fractionation, is necessary to identify porphyria cutanea tarda as the underlying cause of the skin lesions. Water-soluble carboxylated porphyrins in the urine indicate porphyria cutanea tarda or hepatoerythropoietic porphyria, whereas elevated coproporphyrin alone does not. Hepatoerythropoietic porphyria can be distinguished from porphyria cutanea tarda by markedly elevated erythrocyte protoporphyrin levels.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r33">[33]</xref> In addition, the plasma fluorescence peak wavelength at neutral pH can help differentiate porphyria cutanea tarda from variegate porphyria, which is often mistaken for other porphyrias at initial diagnosis.</p>
        <p>Porphyrin levels can also be assessed in a patient&#x02019;s blood, urine, and stool to support diagnosis. In porphyria cutanea tarda, urine typically shows elevated uroporphyrin and coproporphyrin with normal porphobilinogen and delta-aminolevulinic acid. Stool samples show increased uroporphyrin, isocoproporphyrin, and protoporphyrin, while red blood cells usually demonstrate normal levels of uroporphyrin, coproporphyrin, and porphobilinogen. Plasma often reveals elevated uroporphyrin.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r16">[16]</xref>&#x000a0;Skin biopsy is generally not required for diagnosis but may help exclude other cutaneous porphyrias or dermatologic conditions. Histologic findings may include subepidermal blisters and porphyrin deposition in vessel walls.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r34">[34]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27441.s9" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>The first step in the management of PCT is the avoidance of precipitating and susceptibility factors.&#x000a0;Because PCT is a photosensitive skin condition, sunlight avoidance is&#x000a0;essential until porphyrin levels have normalized. Because wavelengths that induce porphyrins are in the range of 400 to 410 nm, only sunscreens containing titanium dioxide or zinc oxide are effective.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r16">[16]</xref>&#x000a0;Sun-protective clothing is also&#x000a0;beneficial. Affected skin areas should be kept clean to prevent infection, and associated pain can be managed with oral analgesics. Other susceptibility factors that should be avoided include alcohol use, smoking, and estrogen therapy, as well as excessive iron intake. The mainstay therapies for PCT are phlebotomy and hydroxychloroquine, and the choice between them depends on the patient&#x02019;s specific comorbidities and susceptibility factors.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Phlebotomy</bold>
</p>
        <p>Any condition leading to iron overload is a clear indication for phlebotomy, which is generally preferred over hydroxychloroquine, although some sources suggest that phlebotomy is equally effective as a low dose of hydroxychloroquine.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r21">[21]</xref> Remission is typically expected within&#x000a0;6 to 9 months.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r35">[35]</xref> Various protocols have been used, such as removing 1 unit (450 mL) of blood every 2 weeks. Strict serial monitoring of ferritin levels is performed to achieve a downward trend until the serum ferritin level falls below 20 ng/mL.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r36">[36]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r37">[37]</xref> Alternatively, removal of 300 milliliters of blood weekly can be used as a treatment strategy.&#x000a0;Care should be taken to avoid inducing anemia or reducing hemoglobin below 10 gm/dL. Skin manifestations typically resolve within four months, although porphyrin levels may take up to 12 months to normalize. Serum ferritin can serve as an early indicator for relapse, as changes in ferritin levels precede changes in porphyrin levels.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r26">[26]</xref>&#x000a0;Phlebotomy is contraindicated in patients with pulmonary or coronary artery disease.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Hydroxychloroquine or Chloroquine</bold>
</p>
        <p>Hydroxychloroquine and chloroquine, which are preferred in patients with ferritin levels below 600 ng/mL or those without an HFE mutation, act by inhibiting the formation and secretion of porphyrins.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r26">[26]</xref> Chloroquine 125 mg administered twice weekly is the preferred therapy, with hydroxychloroquine 200 mg twice weekly used in cases of chloroquine intolerance. Hydroxychloroquine is generally regarded as safer than chloroquine with respect to retinal toxicity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r38">[38]</xref> These regimens may require&#x000a0;6 to 9 months to achieve complete remission. Contraindications include pregnancy, liver disease, preexisting retinal disease, and the concomitant use of other hepatotoxic drugs. Regular monitoring with ophthalmologic examinations to detect retinopathy, the precursor of visual loss, and liver function tests is required. The development of cardiomyopathy, neuromyopathy, or hepatotoxicity, although rare, should prompt discontinuation of the drug or a substantial dose reduction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r39">[39]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r40">[40]</xref>&#x000a0;Close clinical monitoring of labs and patient status is also required.&#x000a0;&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Chelation Therapy</bold>
</p>
        <p>Iron chelation has been considered as an alternative therapy for patients with iron overload&#x02013;induced PCT; however, comparative studies with phlebotomy and hydroxychloroquine have shown it to be less effective. Deferoxamine and deferasirox may be used in patients with contraindications to both phlebotomy and hydroxychloroquine. In addition to the high cost associated with the use of a subcutaneous pump, chelation therapy has the disadvantage of often failing to normalize porphyrin levels even after 12 months of treatment.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27441.s10" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>The most important differentials in a patient with suspected porphyria cutanea tarda include hereditary coproporphyria, variegate porphyria, and congenital erythropoietic porphyria, as all can present with cutaneous manifestations and elevated porphyrin levels. Each of these conditions, however, exhibits a specific pattern of porphyrin accumulation that can be diagnostic; for example, variegate porphyria is characterized by increased urinary levels of coproporphyrins, ALA, and porphobilinogen, whereas congenital erythropoietic porphyria typically presents with normal urinary&#x000a0;ALA and porphobilinogen. Clinically, variegate porphyria and hereditary coproporphyria can be distinguished from PCT by the presence of neurovisceral symptoms, which are absent in patients with PCT.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r16">[16]</xref></p>
        <p>Blistering skin diseases such as epidermolysis bullosa, polymorphous light eruptions, and pseudoporphyria cutanea tarda can appear clinically similar to PCT but can be differentiated through laboratory evaluation, as they do not cause a rise in porphyrin levels. Pseudoporphyria cutanea tarda is clinically and histologically indistinguishable from PCT, yet it occurs without porphyrin elevation and is associated with medications including nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, furosemide, certain antibiotics, and retinoids.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r16">[16]</xref> Epidermolysis bullosa acquisita, an autoimmune blistering disorder, presents with widespread blisters that are not limited to sun-exposed areas, distinguishing it from PCT.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r41">[41]</xref> Polymorphous light eruption manifests as a pruritic, photodistributed rash with erythematous papules, vesicles, and plaques that develop within minutes to hours after sun exposure and do not result in scarring.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r42">[42]</xref></p>
        <p>Bullous systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) presents with widespread bullae that heal without scarring or milia, often demonstrating a neutrophil-predominant infiltrate on biopsy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r43">[43]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r13">[13]</xref> It is a rare manifestation, occurring in less than 5% of SLE patients, and results from autoantibodies targeting type VII collagen or other components of the dermal-epidermal junction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r44">[44]</xref> Genetic studies have not established a definitive link between SLE and PCT, although both conditions share overlapping immunologic features and have been studied in relation to the 1q41&#x02013;q42 locus and the increasing incidence of PCT.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r45">[45]</xref>&#x000a0;Porphyrin elevation may also be observed in patients with liver disease, advanced renal disease, or other porphyrias; however, these conditions do not present with the characteristic cutaneous findings seen in PCT. Therefore, laboratory testing for porphyrin levels, in combination with clinical assessment of cutaneous lesions, remains essential for accurate differentiation.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27441.s11" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>Life expectancy in patients with PCT is generally normal, unless comorbid conditions such as&#x000a0;HCV infection lead to liver disease or carcinoma. Full remission is achievable; however, recurrence is possible, with relapses reported in up to 35% of patients over follow-up periods of up to 11 years.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r46">[46]</xref> Patients with elevated iron levels may require periodic phlebotomy, and those who continue to be exposed to susceptibility factors, such as excessive alcohol consumption or smoking, are at higher risk for relapse. Annual monitoring of urine and plasma uroporphyrin levels is recommended to detect biochemical relapses before clinical manifestations appear.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r47">[47]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27441.s12" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Although the clinical manifestations of PCT are limited to the skin, patients are also at a high risk for hepatic complications. Liver biopsies usually reveal fatty changes along with porphyrin deposits and sometimes lobular necrosis. Porphyria cutanea tarda independently increases the risk for liver cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma, which may be accentuated by coexisting&#x000a0;HCV infection, alcoholic hepatitis, or iron overload.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r48">[48]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27441.s13" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Patients should be educated about the full disease process and reassured that PCT is generally nonfatal. The role of phlebotomy and the need for regular monitoring of porphyrin levels should be explained. Emphasis should be placed on avoiding susceptibility factors, including smoking, alcohol use, estrogen therapy, and iron therapies, as well as on sun-protective measures such as sunscreens, hats, and protective clothing. Patients should disclose any history of&#x000a0;HCV or HIV infection and adhere strictly to the treatment regimens prescribed by their physician. Testing for ferritin levels, infections, and relevant genetic markers is an integral part of the management strategy, and patients should be informed about the rationale for these evaluations.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27441.s14" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>Porphyria cutanea tarda is the most common porphyria and results from decreased activity of the uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase enzyme, leading to the accumulation of porphyrins. Alcohol use, estrogen therapy, viral infections, smoking, and iron overload are susceptibility factors commonly associated with the development of the condition. Excess circulating porphyrins react with light, causing a delayed skin reaction that manifests as blisters, hypertrichosis, hyperpigmentation, and sclerodermalike changes on sun-exposed areas of the skin. Management of&#x000a0;PCT may involve avoidance of precipitating factors, although phlebotomy and hydroxychloroquine are both effective in achieving biochemical and clinical remission.&#x000a0;&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27441.s15" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Porphyria cutanea tarda is one of several porphyrias resulting from decreased activity of an enzyme in the heme biosynthesis pathway. Patients typically present with blistering skin lesions and characteristically lack neurovisceral symptoms. Establishing the diagnosis requires urinalysis, stool analysis, and blood testing as well as a detailed assessment to identify susceptibility factors, such as alcohol use, estrogen therapy, viral infections, smoking, and iron overload, since avoidance of these factors is a critical component of management.</p>
        <p>Although an internist or hematologist usually coordinates care, multiple specialties are involved in the diagnosis and management of PCT. The primary care physician or internist often evaluates the patient first, taking a detailed personal and family history before referring the patient to a dermatologist or hematologist. Dermatologists play a key role in identifying the blistering skin lesions and narrowing the differential diagnosis. While a skin biopsy is not necessary for diagnosis, if performed, the pathologist evaluates the specimen and provides additional diagnostic clues. The hematologist generally oversees the overall evaluation and management plan.</p>
        <p>Laboratory testing of urine, fecal, and blood samples for porphyrin levels is essential to confirm the diagnosis. Porphyrin fractionation may be required to differentiate among porphyrias with similar biochemical profiles. Management typically involves weekly or biweekly phlebotomy and hydroxychloroquine therapy. Phlebotomists, nurses, and patient educators are integral members of the treatment team, with nurses monitoring vital signs, educating patients and families, and supporting adherence to therapy. Complications affecting the liver may necessitate consultation with a hepatologist or gastroenterologist. Counselors assist patients in modifying or eliminating triggers, such as smoking or alcohol use, and genetic counselors may be involved when there is concern for familial risk.</p>
        <p>Porphyria cutanea tarda is associated with an increased risk for hepatocellular and biliary tract cancers. One report indicated that long-term survival in patients with PCT was approximately 50% lower than in the general population over 25 years.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r6">[6]</xref> These findings highlight the importance of early diagnosis, ongoing monitoring, and proactive management.</p>
        <p>Optimal patient outcomes require interprofessional collaboration among primary care physicians, dermatologists, hematologists, nurses, phlebotomists, pathologists, hepatologists, genetic counselors, and patient educators. Effective communication, coordinated care, and patient-centered counseling are essential for improving adherence, preventing relapses, managing comorbidities, and enhancing overall safety and quality of care.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27441.r49">[49]</xref>&#x000a0;&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
